1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,204
Chapter three. The magician.

2
00:00:02,554 --> 00:00:07,511
In the audience that night was a stage magician: Georges Méliès.

3
00:00:07,711 --> 00:00:15,927
He wanted to buy the machine from the Lumières. They said no. According to legend, they told him cinema was an invention without a future.

4
00:00:16,127 --> 00:00:20,056
So he got hold of an English projector and turned it into his own camera.

5
00:00:20,256 --> 00:00:28,383
As he told it, one day he was filming a square when the camera jammed for a minute. When projected, an omnibus suddenly turned into a hearse.

6
00:00:28,583 --> 00:00:35,549
He had discovered a trick: if you stop the camera, change something, and keep filming, on screen it looks like magic.

7
00:00:35,749 --> 00:00:45,171
With tricks like that he made people vanish, multiplied heads, and in 1902, sent a spacecraft to the Moon. It lands right in its eye.

8
00:00:45,371 --> 00:00:54,079
It was so successful that in the United States they copied it without permission. One distributor even offered to sell Méliès… his own film.

9
00:00:54,279 --> 00:01:01,959
Over time, audiences grew tired of his tricks and the war ruined him. He ended up selling toys and candy at a Paris train station.

10
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:10,777
Journalists rediscovered him. In 1931 he received the Legion of Honor, presented by Louis Lumière himself.

11
00:01:11,677 --> 00:01:17,750
The Lumières filmed reality. Méliès filmed dreams. Cinema needed them both.
